---
title: "Reading settings"
---

This page is a reading surface. The **Aa** control (bottom-right of the built page) lets a
reader pick a text size, a reading width, and a theme (light, dark, or sepia). The choice is
stored in the reader's own browser and applied before the next page paints, so it never
flashes and never touches the author's source.

## Why reader controls

A document is read far more often than it is written, on screens the author never saw: a
phone in bright sun, a wide monitor at night, eyes that want larger type. One light/dark
toggle cannot serve all of them. Reader settings move that last decision, *how the words
look*, from the author to the reader, where it belongs.

The text below is here to look right at every size, width, and theme.

## A paragraph of running prose

The gap between a comfortable read and an uncomfortable one is small and entirely mechanical:
the size of the type, the length of the line, and the contrast of the page. Set the measure
too wide and the eye loses the start of the next line; too narrow and the rhythm breaks every
few words. Sepia exists because a warm, low-contrast page is easier on the eye in dim light
than stark black on white. None of this is decoration. It is the difference between finishing
the chapter and closing the tab.

## A figure

![A calm reading column.](../media/fit-a.png){#fig-reading}

@fig-reading should keep its caption and numbering at every text size.

## Some code and a little math

```python
def comfortable(width_ch: int) -> bool:
    return 45 <= width_ch <= 75
```

Inline math like $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ and a display equation should both scale with the reader's
chosen size:

$$ \text{measure} \approx \frac{\text{line length}}{\text{character width}} $$

## A callout

::: {.callout-note}
Reader preferences are reader-local and read-only. They never write back to the `.tmd`, add
no output format, and ship offline, so they sit cleanly inside qmd-fast's invariants.
:::
